"The letter waw in the expression \"Wal-'Asr\" at the beginning of the surah is for an oath. In the dictionary, the word \"'asr\" has meanings such as: dehr (night and day/time), the two ends of the day, particularly the late afternoon, a group of people, and rain. As a verbal noun, it means to confine, to forbid, and to press and squeeze out juice. The specific time of something is also called \"'asr\". Commentators have primarily focused here on the following meanings: the 'Asr prayer, the late afternoon time, dehr and time, especially the era of the Prophet Muhammad (peace be upon him) and the end times. Ibn Jarir stated that the correct view is that \"'asr\" encompasses all these meanings. For \"'asr\" is a homonymous term (lafz mushtarak), and there is no contextual clue specifying any single one; therefore, it is most correct to apply it to all of them.\n\nThe first view: The 'Asr prayer. Muqatil stated that Allah Almighty swore by the 'Asr prayer. For the \"middle prayer\" (Al-Baqarah 2:238) is the 'Asr prayer and is of great merit. In a hadith, it was stated: \"Whoever misses the 'Asr prayer, it is as though his family and his wealth were destroyed.\" Since the late afternoon is the time when people are most deeply engrossed in worldly affairs, an admonition was made by swearing an oath by this prayer.\n\nThe second view: The late afternoon time. Qatadah stated that just as Allah Almighty swore by the forenoon (duha), He also swore by the late afternoon. In terms of being the end of the day, the late afternoon resembles the final stages of a person's life. If a person does not gain anything at this time, they suffer loss. For this reason, after the deceptiveness of boasting in worldly increase was explained in the previous surah, swearing an oath by the late afternoon while describing the loss of mankind in this surah is of great significance.\n\nThe third view: Dehr (time). Those who say that asr means dehr draw attention to the importance of time. Time encompasses bitter and sweet events, and changes. In a narration from 'Ali, \"asr\" was interpreted as dehr, and by swearing an oath by time, it was recalled that man constantly encounters afflictions. Time itself is full of wondrous things; it is a process between non-existence and existence. Human lifespan is the most precious capital, and every moment of it brings profit or loss. Therefore, by swearing an oath by asr, the value of life is emphasized.\n\nThe fourth view: The era of the Prophet (peace be upon him). This era is the era of prophethood. It encompasses the Era of Bliss from the sending of the Prophet until his passing, as well as the end times that follow. This era is like the summary and the late afternoon of all times. In a hadith, it is related that this Ummah is compared to the late afternoon in relation to the other communities. For this reason, swearing an oath by asr indicates the virtue and importance of this era.\n\nIn conclusion: O Muhammad! By asr in all its meanings, particularly the final era, mankind is in absolute loss. Man's capital is his lifespan; every breath is running out and the reckoning is drawing near. Excepted, however, are those who possess the following four qualities: 1) Those who believe, 2) Those who do righteous deeds, 3) Those who advise one another to truth, 4) Those who advise one another to patience. These are not in loss, but in profit. Faith and righteous deeds are for a person's self-perfection, while advising to truth and patience is for perfecting others as well. Patience is divided into two: enduring pain for obedience and struggle, and refraining from forbidden things. These four qualities are the signs of complete faith in the Truth. Those who do not have these attributes are in ruin; and those who believe but do not act are also in a kind of loss. The explanation of the surah encompasses the entire Quran. The Companions would not part upon meeting without reciting this surah. How great indeed is our need to reflect upon our era and advise one another to truth and patience!"